1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,580
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:03,550
I'm Ashwin Krishnappa Kumar.

3
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:07,849
Today, I wanna talk about a problem
that sits, right where security,

4
00:00:07,869 --> 00:00:10,299
data, and user experience meet.

5
00:00:10,969 --> 00:00:13,889
Securing real-time
analytics on mobile devices.

6
00:00:14,499 --> 00:00:17,810
One quick note before we begin,
the views in this talk are my own.

7
00:00:18,249 --> 00:00:22,029
They have not been reviewed or
approved by Tesla Let's talk about

8
00:00:22,039 --> 00:00:23,459
the expanding attack surface.

9
00:00:23,669 --> 00:00:25,749
so it's a very simple observation.

10
00:00:26,079 --> 00:00:31,239
the people who run operations, people
who lead teams, who manage teams,

11
00:00:31,289 --> 00:00:36,179
executives, are no longer just checking
dashboards from a laptop at their desk.

12
00:00:36,739 --> 00:00:40,679
They're checking from their phones,
on the floor, in a meeting, at home.

13
00:00:41,339 --> 00:00:43,419
Now, this is a very
good thing for business.

14
00:00:43,589 --> 00:00:48,239
Decisions get made faster, but it
changes the security picture completely.

15
00:00:49,159 --> 00:00:53,289
The attack surface grows before
a single dashboard even loads.

16
00:00:54,119 --> 00:00:59,109
Device access to operational data is
now the norm, and it's not an exception.

17
00:01:00,229 --> 00:01:04,519
The perimeter controls most of us
rely on, were never designed for it.

18
00:01:06,349 --> 00:01:08,739
So one design problem, and it's not three.

19
00:01:09,939 --> 00:01:15,469
When, teams tackle these specific security
issues, they usually split in three ways.

20
00:01:16,129 --> 00:01:21,149
The identity team handles login, the
data team handles the data permissions,

21
00:01:21,649 --> 00:01:23,769
the app team handles performance.

22
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:30,910
When these problems are split between
teams, it's not easily compatible.

23
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:36,439
My argument today is that this split
is exactly where the gaps come from.

24
00:01:37,269 --> 00:01:40,880
Identity, authorization, and
performance are all one design problem.

25
00:01:42,149 --> 00:01:47,459
If we solve them separately, we leave
seams, and attackers find seams.

26
00:01:49,439 --> 00:01:53,239
So during this session, the roadmap
is just going to be I'll be walking

27
00:01:53,239 --> 00:01:58,910
through five areas: identifying,
binding or unmanaged devices, fast

28
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:04,180
role-based authorization, integrating
data across domains, keeping data fresh

29
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:09,389
without stale permissions, and finally,
the new challenge, AI-driven access.

30
00:02:10,389 --> 00:02:11,769
Enterprise identity binding.

31
00:02:12,729 --> 00:02:13,979
First is identity.

32
00:02:14,609 --> 00:02:15,519
Here's a core principle.

33
00:02:16,659 --> 00:02:20,689
A personal phone touching operational
data must carry the same trust

34
00:02:20,689 --> 00:02:23,099
guarantee as a managed corporate laptop.

35
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:29,259
But we can't ask everyone to enroll their
personal phone in device management.

36
00:02:29,329 --> 00:02:31,369
People won't do it, and
they shouldn't have to.

37
00:02:32,329 --> 00:02:36,189
So instead of managing the device,
we bind identity in layers.

38
00:02:37,129 --> 00:02:39,949
At the base is device attestation.

39
00:02:40,269 --> 00:02:45,409
We verify the OS hasn't been tampered
with, and we use hardware-backed keys.

40
00:02:46,379 --> 00:02:50,839
On top of that is sec-- is
corporate identity federation.

41
00:02:51,829 --> 00:02:54,569
Single sign-on with
certified backed tokens.

42
00:02:55,519 --> 00:02:58,309
And at the top is continuous
session validation.

43
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:01,889
We don't check trust once
at login and walk away.

44
00:03:01,889 --> 00:03:05,789
We reevaluate the risk
signals on every request.

45
00:03:06,949 --> 00:03:09,269
Trust isn't a gate you pass once.

46
00:03:09,339 --> 00:03:13,749
It's an envelope that travels
with every call Authorization

47
00:03:13,749 --> 00:03:16,249
on mobile, at mobile speed.

48
00:03:16,749 --> 00:03:19,729
Now authorization, and here's
where performance comes in.

49
00:03:20,429 --> 00:03:24,959
On a desktop, a half-second
permission check is barely noticed.

50
00:03:25,359 --> 00:03:26,919
On a phone, it feels broken.

51
00:03:27,859 --> 00:03:31,069
Users will abandon a slow app
or worse, find workarounds.

52
00:03:32,259 --> 00:03:35,389
So how do we stay strict
without being slow?

53
00:03:36,329 --> 00:03:40,369
Three mechanisms One is
cache permission checks.

54
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:46,109
We pre-compute role resolution and
cache it at the edge, key to the

55
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:48,579
identity token and the data scope.

56
00:03:49,199 --> 00:03:50,869
No round trip on every render.

57
00:03:52,329 --> 00:03:56,129
Two, TTL-bounded invalidation.

58
00:03:56,129 --> 00:03:58,939
Every cache entry has
a strict time to live.

59
00:03:59,569 --> 00:04:03,349
When a role or a policy changes,
the cache is forced to revalidate.

60
00:04:04,409 --> 00:04:06,329
Stale permissions don't linger.

61
00:04:07,699 --> 00:04:09,619
Three, fail closed.

62
00:04:09,819 --> 00:04:14,439
If there's a cache miss or the network
is degraded, the answer is deny.

63
00:04:14,839 --> 00:04:15,409
Never open.

64
00:04:17,129 --> 00:04:22,499
The device context demands the strictness,
the mobile experience demands the speed.

65
00:04:22,509 --> 00:04:27,699
Caching the permission decision,
not the data, gives us both

66
00:04:28,699 --> 00:04:30,749
The third area is cross-domain data.

67
00:04:31,439 --> 00:04:36,159
A single mobile dashboard might pull
data from various different departments.

68
00:04:36,969 --> 00:04:40,629
Each one of it has its own
owner and its own access policy.

69
00:04:41,629 --> 00:04:45,639
The common mistake is to join all
the data together, then hide what

70
00:04:45,639 --> 00:04:47,429
the user shouldn't see in the UI.

71
00:04:48,409 --> 00:04:52,979
That's enforcing policy at the
presentation layer, and it means

72
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:57,989
sensitive data is already sitting
in a payload on a personal device.

73
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:02,269
The rule is simple: enforce
policy where the data is

74
00:05:02,269 --> 00:05:04,319
joined, not when it's displayed

75
00:05:05,319 --> 00:05:07,309
The multi-drill down problem.

76
00:05:08,789 --> 00:05:14,509
This gets sharper when users drill down
At the core is the enterprise summary.

77
00:05:14,969 --> 00:05:16,789
Broad authorization is enough there.

78
00:05:17,889 --> 00:05:21,539
Tap into a facility or a
department, and we revalidate

79
00:05:21,809 --> 00:05:23,539
regional and departmental scope.

80
00:05:24,619 --> 00:05:28,779
Tap again into individual performance
data, and we do a final check

81
00:05:29,439 --> 00:05:30,969
before revealing any record.

82
00:05:32,329 --> 00:05:32,729
Why?

83
00:05:33,089 --> 00:05:37,479
Because a broad grant at the
top should never quickly unlock

84
00:05:37,559 --> 00:05:39,319
restricted detail at the bottom.

85
00:05:40,449 --> 00:05:41,399
Every level revalidates

86
00:05:42,399 --> 00:05:45,339
Now let's talk about freshness
and permission consistency.

87
00:05:46,819 --> 00:05:51,639
Freshness is basically about how
quickly we can refresh that data.

88
00:05:52,479 --> 00:05:56,759
Operation teams want data refreshed
every second or every minute.

89
00:05:57,169 --> 00:06:02,449
That's great, but speed can never come at
the cost of a wrongly scoped permission.

90
00:06:03,889 --> 00:06:08,379
So every refresh cycle is paired with
an authorization consistency check.

91
00:06:09,969 --> 00:06:15,649
Three pieces make that work A distributed
warehouse where queries are scoped

92
00:06:16,289 --> 00:06:18,379
at read time, not at batch time.

93
00:06:19,069 --> 00:06:22,859
So when access changes, the next
query reflects it immediately.

94
00:06:24,349 --> 00:06:26,529
Permission stamped data tokens.

95
00:06:26,989 --> 00:06:30,679
Every payload carries an
authorization fingerprint.

96
00:06:31,409 --> 00:06:36,239
If the policy changes mid-session,
the fingerprint is invalidated.

97
00:06:37,559 --> 00:06:39,609
And push notification gating.

98
00:06:40,389 --> 00:06:45,219
Alerts are permission-checked on
the server before they're sent.

99
00:06:45,789 --> 00:06:49,739
Nothing reaches the device until
authorization confirms it should

100
00:06:50,739 --> 00:06:52,139
AI-augmented insight delivery.

101
00:06:53,599 --> 00:06:56,619
Finally, the area that's
changing faster is AI.

102
00:06:57,269 --> 00:07:01,739
Natural language queries and AI-generated
alerts are a huge step forward in

103
00:07:01,759 --> 00:07:06,249
how people reach data, but they
break assumptions that traditional

104
00:07:06,299 --> 00:07:08,429
role-based access was built on.

105
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:14,289
An AI summary can aggregate data across
scope boundaries without anyone noticing.

106
00:07:15,209 --> 00:07:17,879
A natural language question is unbounded.

107
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:23,569
You can't predict what someone will
ask, and a RAG pipeline that searches

108
00:07:23,639 --> 00:07:28,799
everything and filters afterwards has
already touched the data it shouldn't

109
00:07:29,799 --> 00:07:30,559
So what do we do?

110
00:07:30,959 --> 00:07:32,539
Query scope authorization.

111
00:07:33,079 --> 00:07:35,879
So we authorize the
query, not just the user.

112
00:07:36,799 --> 00:07:38,249
And this has four steps.

113
00:07:38,739 --> 00:07:40,419
The user asks a question.

114
00:07:40,819 --> 00:07:45,289
Before any retrieval happens, their
identity token is mapped to the

115
00:07:45,289 --> 00:07:47,129
data domains they're allowed to see.

116
00:07:48,149 --> 00:07:54,059
Retrieval then runs only against those
authorized partitions, including at the

117
00:07:54,059 --> 00:07:58,959
vector search layer, and the response is
generated from the scoped context alone.

118
00:08:00,329 --> 00:08:04,179
The key point, this is a
first-class architectural concern.

119
00:08:04,579 --> 00:08:10,619
It is not a filter you bolt onto
the AI's output after the fact

120
00:08:11,619 --> 00:08:13,309
So four takeaways in general.

121
00:08:13,359 --> 00:08:16,989
One, bind identity at the device layer.

122
00:08:17,309 --> 00:08:22,829
Hardware attestation plus federated
SSO makes unmanaged devices

123
00:08:23,209 --> 00:08:26,309
trustworthy without MDM enrollment.

124
00:08:27,289 --> 00:08:29,899
Two, cache permissions, not data.

125
00:08:30,479 --> 00:08:35,669
Edge-resolved DTL-bounded caches
give you role-based access at mobile

126
00:08:35,909 --> 00:08:38,859
speed, and they'll fail closed.

127
00:08:40,639 --> 00:08:47,349
Three, enforce policy at integration,
not presentation, and revalidate

128
00:08:47,349 --> 00:08:49,209
scope at every drill-down level.

129
00:08:50,469 --> 00:08:56,839
Four, authorize the query, not
just the user, for AI and RAG.

130
00:08:56,839 --> 00:08:59,959
Enforce scope at retrieval, not at output

131
00:09:00,959 --> 00:09:01,339
Thank you

